WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.395
GGUF is a model container commonly used by llama.cpp and compatible local runtimes.

00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:09.520
It can package quantized tensors together with model architecture information and other

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.600
metadata needed for loading. A single model family may be published as many GGUF files,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.730
each using a different quantization method or size. The file name is useful,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.840
but runtime support and embedded metadata determine whether it can be loaded correctly.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.040
Safetensors is a safe,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.840
efficient tensor-storage format widely used in model repositories and framework workflows.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.740
Safetensors files may contain full-precision, half-precision,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.440
or quantized tensors depending on the model and loader.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.140
The extension alone does not tell you the numerical precision,

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.920
and it does not guarantee that a local runtime supports the model architecture or

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.595
quantization scheme. Models often require more than weight files.

00:00:43.595 --> 00:00:47.400
Tokenizer vocabulary, tokenizer configuration, special-token definitions,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.455
generation settings, prompt templates,

00:00:49.455 --> 00:00:53.770
and architecture configuration influence how text becomes tokens and how conversations are

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:54.480
formatted.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.550
Mixing weights with an incompatible tokenizer or template can produce poor output even

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.400
when the model loads successfully.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.205
Before downloading a variant, identify the intended runtime, architecture support,

00:01:04.205 --> 00:01:07.480
quantization method, context requirements, and available memory.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.400
Confirm provenance and license terms.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.470
The same base model can appear in multiple containers and many quantized variants because

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.920
different hardware, runtimes, and quality targets need different tradeoffs.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.410
Format is part of the compatibility chain, not a universal indicator of quality.
